WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.840
Please welcome Kygo.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.960
-Hi, welcome.
-Thank you.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.640
For exact a year ago, 
you had never entered a stage.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.160
No, that's right.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.680
In January, 
I did my first concert in Paris.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.440
-Did you know what to do then? 
-No, not really.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.880
I've got the offer to come to Paris 
for one and a half year ago.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.800
I had to turn it down because 
I didn't know how to DJ on stage.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.840
I had never before 
touch a DJ mixing console.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.160
Your entire career has been 
taking place at the Internet.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.280
Yes, via social medias, 
Facebook and SoundCloud.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:07.280
That's a site for distributing music, 
and then people might like or share.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.120
I've started to make remixes,

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.360
alternative versions of known 
and less known tunes.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.200
Then people shared it 
with their friends, and so on.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:27.640
-But you studied something else? 
-I've started economics in Edinburgh.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.720
I really wanted to work with music,

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.600
but I heard it was so difficult.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.120
No one is breaking through. 
Lots of people do what I do.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.640
-The two of you studied that as well? 
-Yes.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.480
What is it about studying economics,
but not wanting to work with it?

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.040
I also wondering.
At that time, I've always wondered: 

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.360
"What kind of 
economist job is right for me?"

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.560
"What would suit me?"

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.120
But it's a broad range of areas.
Apart from calculations,

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.360
 it's about society, 
and you got to know people.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.120
Then you have to figure out 
what you want to do.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.880
How do you...

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.320
You might think I'm 80 years old now 
but you remix tunes...

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.680
...that even we are familiar with.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.560
Marvin Gaye, Dolly Parton... 
What more? Coldplay...

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.960
Yes and Ed Sheeran.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:34.200
I like to find older tunes 
and blow a new life in to them.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.320
I want to create modern versions.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:42.760
As I did with Marvin Gaye's 
"Sexual Healing".

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.440
Are you just able to take his tune 
and add things to that,

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.000
or do you having...?

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.200
In that case, I've found a clip 
of an acappella version at YouTube.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.680
A clip there Marvin Gaye 
sings without any instruments.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.200
Then it was easy for me 
to make my own arrangement.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.320
Add instruments like piano, bass 
and drums. Or whatever I like to add.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.800
Are they proud of you home in Bergen?

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.440
I've get lots of positive response. 
Both from family and friends.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:25.000
Even from random people in the streets. 
It's fun to get support from home.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:33.080
When the Mayor of Bergen was told 
that you would come here tonight, 

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.760
she wanted to send you a greeting.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.400
-Alright... 
-... An appeal.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.680
I would like to show it before 
you leave for the stage.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.800
-That's great to hear. 
-Look at this.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.560
Kygo means a lot for the town.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:04:00.480
But I think that the remix 
now got a bit too far.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.880
The church bells doesn't sound 
the way they used to.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.960
The police cars don't go 
"ULI ULI" anymore.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.560
We can't keep on like this. 
All sounds in our city are remixed.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.920
There have to be just two sounds.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:28.320
The original sound 
and the remixed sound.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.240
And they have to be presented 
in a clear manner.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.760
I'm quite confident that 
Kygo and myself, together,

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.840
will sort out this remix-epidemic.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.320
I am sitting here and Kygo here. 
Right beside me.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.120
That was the Major of Bergen, 
Trude Drevland.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.400
That was awesome. 
I had no idea about that.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.600
She's sitting there and waiting for you.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.640
But first you will take the stage.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.280
You will perform something 
special for us today, 

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.320
where you actually play piano.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.400
Yes, we thought we would do 
a special version today.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:19.640
It's from my new single "Firestone" 
featured by Conrad, an Australian singer.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:25.040
Originally it's electronic with a lot 
of bass, but this version is acoustic.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.480
So It's only me at the piano, 
Conrad on the vocal and a drum player.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:34.840
-It will be a stripped version.
-They are waiting for you on stage. 

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.720
-Thanks a lot for coming.
-Thanks for letting me.

